// NEGATIVE TEST — a literal pattern is an EQUALITY TEST, so its type is the
// scrutinee's (N6-02), and `char` is not `i64` (N4-04).
//
// This is the pattern-position half of N4-04's no-implicit-conversion rule. In
// C both sides are a `long long` and the comparison would compile and answer, so
// nothing downstream would object: `match n { 'a' => … }` on an `i64` would
// silently test `n == 97`. The type checker is the only place this can be caught,
// which is why the row exists.
fn main() {
    let n: i64 = 5;
    let v = match n {
        'a' => 1,
        _ => 0,
    };
    print_int(v);
}
